WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.380
&gt;&gt; It's my great pleasure

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.690
to introduce David Sontag

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.515
as our first speaker
of the session.

00:00:11.515 --> 00:00:14.750
And, actually, I first

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:18.300
met him in New York when
we were both with NYU.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.420
I now notice that
you are now with

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.100
MIT as an Assistant Professor.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.650
Already when I was with NYU,

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.460
I was very impressed by

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.170
how he applies

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.100
very interesting innovative
machine learning techniques

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.510
to a domain that we probably,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.420
now or at some point,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.585
care about and
that is healthcare.

00:00:42.585 --> 00:00:44.320
And, yeah, I'm looking forward

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.135
to see what you're
working on these days.

00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:49.600
&gt;&gt; Well, thanks very much for

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.665
the organizers for inviting me.

00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:53.440
In fact, they didn't invite me.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.460
They invited Anna Choromanska
to give this talk.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.265
They invited me to
give another talk,

00:00:59.265 --> 00:01:01.970
but Anna unfortunately
couldn't make it today.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:04.385
She has a young baby at home.

00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:06.610
And so today I'll be

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.705
taking her place to
talk about this work.

00:01:08.705 --> 00:01:11.440
It's joint work between myself,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.220
Yacine Jernite who was
a PhD student of mine at

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.470
NYU, and Anna Choromanska.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.870
And, reall, y Yacine,

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.920
the chunk of these slides,
he couldn't make it either,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.840
but really the credit
should go to him.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.285
This work had appeared
in our ICML 2017 paper.

00:01:35.285 --> 00:01:38.850
So, the high-level questions
are as follows.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.500
How can we do extreme
classification quickly?

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.930
This is the topic which has been

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.055
discussed already
quite a bit this morning.

00:01:47.055 --> 00:01:49.270
And for us, we've been very

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.040
much motivated by
language modeling,

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.660
although the techniques I'll
tell you about today will

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.240
be really applicable to

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.600
many other settings
such as classification.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.595
So, language modeling, we
would like given some text

00:02:04.595 --> 00:02:07.130
to learn a distribution

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.145
over text such that
we could model,

00:02:10.145 --> 00:02:13.385
for example, draw
the new sentence from the model,

00:02:13.385 --> 00:02:15.770
or we could ask given
some existing words

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.540
what's the likelihood of
seeing some of the next word.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.210
So, let's look at
this simple example

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.235
here. The rats scared the cat.

00:02:22.235 --> 00:02:25.715
So, if we had just seen
the first four words,

00:02:25.715 --> 00:02:27.140
the rat scared the,

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.970
we might ask what
is the likelihood of

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:31.865
the next word in that sentence,
in this case, cat.

00:02:31.865 --> 00:02:33.655
Well, by the chain rule,

00:02:33.655 --> 00:02:37.000
any set of words, w1 through wn,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.600
the distribution
over them could be

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.280
factorized as
probability of word one,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.020
probability of word
two given word one,

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.340
probability of word
three given word

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.890
one comma word two and so
on, just by the chain rule.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.860
So, in order to compute
each conditional likelihood,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.620
we need to get some distribution
over the next word,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.785
given all the previous words.

00:02:57.785 --> 00:03:00.360
Neural language models typically

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.700
summarize those previous words
with some context vector,

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.120
in this case, we'll refer
to that as a v context.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.795
And it might, for example,

00:03:08.795 --> 00:03:10.900
be done by taking

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.620
word embeddings for each of
the words in the sentence,

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.120
pushing them through a recurrent

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.280
neural network and looking at

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.970
the hidden state
at the last word,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.440
which in this case is the.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.830
And that would summarize
all the previous words

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.735
in some D-dimensional vector.

00:03:25.735 --> 00:03:27.590
And given that
D-dimensional vector,

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.755
then the question is
what is that next word?

00:03:29.755 --> 00:03:32.955
We want to predict one class

00:03:32.955 --> 00:03:35.600
out of a class of size
the whole vocabulary,

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.205
which could be in a millions.

00:03:38.205 --> 00:03:41.140
Now, often that
conditional distribution of

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.180
that last word p of cat
given the context v,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.060
the rat scared the,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.410
would be parametrized
with a softmax function.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.555
So it would look
something like this,

00:03:52.555 --> 00:03:55.455
where we say that for
every word in the vocabulary,

00:03:55.455 --> 00:03:57.570
we have some vector.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.335
So, that's here denoted as a uw,

00:04:00.335 --> 00:04:02.080
which is also D-dimensional,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.640
and then the conditional
distribution of w

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.280
given the context is
just given to you by

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.160
a renormalization of
the exponential dot-product

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.790
of the context with
that word vector uw,

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:17.930
renormalized so that we have

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.800
a valid distribution
over all possible words.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.150
And this is nothing new. This
is the standard approach to

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:23.720
language modelling that's used

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.470
across natural
language processing.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.065
Now, to compute
that numerator, for example,

00:04:29.065 --> 00:04:33.010
that exponential term, it
takes running time order D,

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.340
where D is the dimensionality of

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.285
that context and word embedding.

00:04:37.285 --> 00:04:39.010
But in order to compute

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.855
the full conditional
distribution,

00:04:40.855 --> 00:04:42.880
we need that
denominator as well.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.800
So, it's going to be order D

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.990
time to compute
each of these terms,

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:52.540
and then the denominator is
going to be order D times v,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.160
the vocabulary size
running time,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.350
because we have to sum
over all possible words in

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:57.740
the vocabulary to get a valid

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.755
distribution over
the vocabulary.

00:04:59.755 --> 00:05:03.820
And that's the problem.
That ends up being

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.730
one of the main bottlenecks

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:08.550
for learning
neural language models.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.490
So, there have been
a number of approaches in

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.640
the NLP community for trying to

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.090
address this bottleneck
during training.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:20.290
You might have heard of
things like negative sampling,

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:24.110
self-normalizing models,
and hierarchical softmax.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.920
And in our work, we're
going to be focused on

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.200
that last approach,
hierarchical softmax.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.880
And one of the really
nice things about

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.410
this hierarchical softmax
is that it gives us

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:35.880
a valid language model
so we can exactly

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.910
compute the probability of
any next word in the sentence.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.670
And it's going to be fast,

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.220
not just at training but also at

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.320
test time that some of

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.240
the other approaches
might not have.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.480
So, what is this hierarchical

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.240
softmax that I'm talking about?

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.730
Well, the key idea is rather

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.860
than describing the distribution

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:58.320
over all of the words
using a softmax,

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.820
we're going to predict first.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.410
We're going to, in essence,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.735
follow the trajectory
through a tree,

00:06:06.735 --> 00:06:09.170
and only when we
get to the leaf of

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.100
the tree will we know what

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.740
is the word that
we want to predict.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.565
And at each step,

00:06:13.565 --> 00:06:15.380
we're going to be
just making binary,

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.325
or in our work, it'll
be M-ary decisions.

00:06:18.325 --> 00:06:20.800
And so the total running time
in order to make

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.320
a prediction is going to be

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.490
much smaller than
the vocabulary size,

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:27.150
potentially logarithmic
in the vocabulary size.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.675
Let's see how this works.

00:06:29.675 --> 00:06:32.230
Let's stick with
the previous example.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.160
We want to figure out,
given that context,

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.250
what is the likelihood of
the next word being cat.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.770
Well, the first thing
we're going to do is we're

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:44.185
going to look at
the root of the tree,

00:06:44.185 --> 00:06:47.620
and we're going to ask what

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.690
is the likelihood of
branching, in this case,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:51.780
it's a three-area tree,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.565
so what's the likelihood
of branching to

00:06:53.565 --> 00:06:56.980
n2 as being the next step
in this procedure.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:00.050
And I'm going to be asking about

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:01.670
each one of these steps
until you get to

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:04.155
the leaf corresponding
to the label cat.

00:07:04.155 --> 00:07:06.410
So, we're going to factorize.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:07.730
We're going to stipulate that

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.960
the distribution of cat
given the context is now

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.140
going to be factorized

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.450
as each decision has to be
made to get to that leaf.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:18.410
So first, probability
of n2 given n1,

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.380
then probability of n4 given n2,

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.180
then probability
of cat given n4.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.710
And if you multiply each of
the probabilities together,

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.410
you get the overall likelihood

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.210
of getting to the label cat.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.440
You can now imagine
a generative process

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.340
where we want to
sample a new word,

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:36.290
given this conditional context.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.300
We're going to sample from

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:39.860
that first distribution
and sort of work

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.220
our way through that tree
to get to the leaf.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:48.595
So, the running time
then goes from, well,

00:07:48.595 --> 00:07:51.480
at each decision, it
takes running time D

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.030
times m where m is

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.860
again the branching factor
of this tree.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:57.685
D is the dimensionality
of the context.

00:07:57.685 --> 00:08:03.610
And if the tree
is of depth log v,

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.000
the vocabulary size, meaning
it's a balanced tree,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.785
then the running time
is now logarithmic

00:08:07.785 --> 00:08:10.810
in the vocabulary size
in order to sample from

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.090
the conditional distribution
or to estimate

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:16.395
the conditional likelihood
of a particular label,

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:17.690
in this case, the label again

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:20.955
corresponds to a word
in the vocabulary.

00:08:20.955 --> 00:08:26.900
So, this hierarchical softmax
is widely used in NLP,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.490
but the question which
everyone in the community

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.870
always asks is what
should that tree be?

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:33.790
What is its structure?
Where is it coming from?

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:35.890
And there have been

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.635
a number of approaches to
try to address that question.

00:08:38.635 --> 00:08:43.730
The first one has been
one of clustering.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:46.470
So, for example, you might

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.950
use off-the-shelf algorithm
like Word2vec to learn

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.080
some word embeddings and
then do some clustering of

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.310
these word embeddings to
get a candidate tree and

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:55.700
then train the parameters of

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.770
that tree or maybe
iterate this procedure in

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.150
some way in order to try to

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:04.380
find trees that are
semantically meaningful,

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.830
that words that might have

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.880
similar meanings maybe
are in similar locations

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.610
along the bottom of the tree.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.370
So, that's one approach.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.510
Another approach
that's widely used

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:23.430
is based on Huffman coding,

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:27.485
which is completely wacky,
except that it works.

00:09:27.485 --> 00:09:31.050
So, this Huffman coding idea
says that we're going

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.835
to completely
ignore the semantics

00:09:33.835 --> 00:09:35.730
of what these labels are.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.320
We're just going to try to learn

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.080
a tree structure such
that very frequent label,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.795
and this won't be
a balanced tree,

00:09:43.795 --> 00:09:47.200
very frequently occurring
labels are high up in the tree,

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.590
and not so frequently incurring

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.690
labels are low in the tree,

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:51.040
so that if you are to

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.440
completely ignore
the contexts and just ask

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.770
the question of what's

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.800
the expected number of

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.185
branches you need to
do to get to a word,

00:10:01.185 --> 00:10:03.620
it's as low as possible.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:08.710
And, finally, in even more
recent work, for example,

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.110
we've used this in my own lab,

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:14.040
people have just gone
for random hierarchies.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.130
Just giving up on
anything and just saying,

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.720
"Okay, we're going to
randomly create a tree."

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.815
So, for example, in my work,

00:10:21.815 --> 00:10:24.940
I've used a service square root

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.480
v tree where we
have a square root

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:35.030
v branching and then
it's not very deep.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:38.480
So we just randomly
assign words to

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.485
the leafs and learn a model,

00:10:41.485 --> 00:10:44.810
and that also seems to
work reasonably well.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:48.460
But this leaves open
the question of, "Okay,

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:50.180
these things work
reasonably well,

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:52.930
but what is there
still to be gained?"

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:54.850
If we were to actually try to

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:56.370
think hard about
this problem and

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.985
learn that tree structure,
could we do better?

00:10:59.985 --> 00:11:02.710
And this is one of the first

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:06.145
works to really
address that problem.

00:11:06.145 --> 00:11:08.270
And the conclusion, I'll
just tell you that now,

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:10.990
the conclusion is that there is

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:16.230
a substantial gap in performance

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.510
between either the
clustering-based approaches or

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:22.420
the Huffman coding-based
approaches and the flat softmax.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:26.735
And by cleverly learning
that tree structure,

00:11:26.735 --> 00:11:29.595
we completely eliminate
that gap, that's the conclusion,

00:11:29.595 --> 00:11:31.270
while still attaining most of

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.730
the computational speedups.
So, how do we get there?

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:37.420
Well, there's been

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.530
quite a bit of
earlier work on learning

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:41.690
tree structures in
similar settings notably

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:45.090
by Choromanska and
John Langford sitting here.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.260
And that itself built on

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:50.670
uneven earlier work by
currents and mentor.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.870
But, they considered
settings where

00:11:53.870 --> 00:12:00.165
their contacts were unchanging.

00:12:00.165 --> 00:12:02.265
So, for example
if you think about

00:12:02.265 --> 00:12:08.700
a typical bag-of-words
classification setting,

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.400
where we have a fixed feature
vector representation,

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:12.280
we're just trying
to learn how to

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:13.440
predict from a large number of

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.660
classes what
the classes should be.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:20.565
Then those algorithms
would apply.

00:12:20.565 --> 00:12:23.015
But here, the story's
a little bit different.

00:12:23.015 --> 00:12:25.780
Because, remember when you're
learning a language model,

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.780
you're not just learning
this tree structure,

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:31.555
but you're also learning

00:12:31.555 --> 00:12:37.790
the recurrent neural network
parameters as well, right?

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:39.090
So, you have to learn
the word embedding.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:43.170
You have to learn the parameters
of your gates and so on.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:46.410
So, what that means is
that the representation

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.660
of your contacts are

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:51.680
changing as well during
your learning algorithm.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.240
And what we don't want to do is

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.830
a two-step algorithm
where you fix

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.780
the representation and learn
the tree and then alternate.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.830
What we want to ask
in this work is is

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:03.140
there a way to jointly
learn everything?

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:06.455
And so we're going to give
an algorithm to do so.

00:13:06.455 --> 00:13:10.875
We're going to provide very
weak theoretical guarantees.

00:13:10.875 --> 00:13:13.100
So, we have some theory
in our paper,

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.185
but really it leaves
a lot to be desired.

00:13:15.185 --> 00:13:19.780
But we do show that this
works very well in practice.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.470
And we're going to
evaluate this in

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.030
both the language
modeling setting,

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.715
and in an extreme
classification setting.

00:13:25.715 --> 00:13:28.180
So, our overall approach
will actually

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.840
be a little bit
similar to how you

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:33.190
would tackle learning
a decision tree, right?

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:34.540
So, if you think about
learning decision tree,

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:35.710
you might think about

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:39.215
some criteria for how you
would build that decision tree.

00:13:39.215 --> 00:13:42.255
You think about
things like purity

00:13:42.255 --> 00:13:48.170
or accuracy as measures for
how to choose the next split.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:49.590
And you might build
that decision tree

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:51.320
in a top-down fashion.

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.350
We're going to give you
the same sort of objective

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:55.790
here except it's going to

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:57.610
be a little bit
different to take into

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:00.660
consideration some of
the subtleties of this problem.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.360
And our learning algorithm

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.075
won't be a greedy
top-down algorithm.

00:14:06.075 --> 00:14:08.855
It's going to have steps in it

00:14:08.855 --> 00:14:11.540
of fitting the language model

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.870
completely redoing
the tree and alternating.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:18.455
And what we're going
to show is that

00:14:18.455 --> 00:14:22.345
using this objective
leads to good trees.

00:14:22.345 --> 00:14:25.850
So, here's now where I get
into some of the details.

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:30.140
So, first I never told
you how we branch.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:33.590
So, let's first look at
the setting of just two classes.

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:34.940
We'll call it the class for

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:37.825
the word lost and
the one for time.

00:14:37.825 --> 00:14:41.830
Suppose, we have some data
with here the data consists of

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:44.440
sentences that give you

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.340
relevant contacts
for what she would

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.260
like to predict that next word.

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:51.315
And let's suppose
that next word was lost or time.

00:14:51.315 --> 00:14:55.690
So, if we just had
two classes then our tree

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:59.690
is just going to be
a binary tree of depth one.

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.580
Where we only have
to make one decision

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:02.930
go left or go right,

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:05.380
in order to get to one
of these two classes.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:07.880
And so there one could use

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:11.440
just a sigmoid function
where you take that context,

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:14.140
and you hit it with

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:15.710
this linear classifier
parameterized

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:17.350
by a weight vector H,

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:19.760
and that gives you
some distribution.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.000
According to
the segment or logistic

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:27.025
over which classes the right
class for that context.

00:15:27.025 --> 00:15:29.800
And if you had more than
two classes that is to

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:34.590
say if rather than
a binary this were

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:41.750
an M area tree then you'll
just see the softmax instead.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:45.700
And so that then gives you
the probability of each one of

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:50.880
these classes given
the relevant context.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:54.880
So, now suppose that

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.615
you have more than
two classes and again,

00:15:57.615 --> 00:15:59.000
for this example,
I'm going to stick

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:01.715
with M equals two
so a binary tree.

00:16:01.715 --> 00:16:08.535
But, in our experiments
would go with M up to 65.

00:16:08.535 --> 00:16:13.575
So, now the story is
going to be as follows.

00:16:13.575 --> 00:16:17.400
Consider the
following data point.

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.855
"I ate some" gives
you some context,

00:16:20.855 --> 00:16:22.720
this is how we do prediction.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:23.760
So, not learning but just

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:24.930
prediction supposing
that we have learned

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:27.780
the model. We start at the root.

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:29.610
The root node is associated with

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.600
some parameters in this case
since it's a binary tree.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.145
There's just a single hyperplane

00:16:35.145 --> 00:16:37.375
we're using linear splits.

00:16:37.375 --> 00:16:39.225
At that hyperplane tells us,

00:16:39.225 --> 00:16:40.710
by taking the dot-product
of the hyperplane with

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:43.535
a context factor and decides
to go left or go right.

00:16:43.535 --> 00:16:46.400
Then here say you go left,

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:49.540
you make the similar decision
now for node n2,

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:51.820
node n4 and
eventually you get to

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:54.110
your prediction which
is the label pie, right?

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:56.030
So, then the likelihood of

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.010
pie is just given to
you by the product of

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:00.345
each one of those softmaxes

00:17:00.345 --> 00:17:02.960
of the decisions needed to
make to reach that label.

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:05.170
So, now let's ask

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.810
the question of "How do we
learn that tree structure?".

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:12.875
Well, we gave an objective
function which will

00:17:12.875 --> 00:17:17.985
quantify how good the split
at any one node is.

00:17:17.985 --> 00:17:20.560
And then, our overall objective
function is going to be

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:25.285
the sum of those
per node objectives.

00:17:25.285 --> 00:17:28.140
So, now I'll tell you

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.690
what each of those per
node objectives is.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:34.155
For each node n in your tree,

00:17:34.155 --> 00:17:39.990
again this is an M-ary tree
with k over all classes,

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:45.540
where k, remember
could be something like

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:46.910
if this was vocabulary,

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:48.765
it would be vocabulary size.

00:17:48.765 --> 00:17:50.680
So, for each node in
the tree we're going to

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.720
look to see; first,

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.515
what are the distribution of

00:17:54.515 --> 00:17:58.370
classes that reach
that node in the tree.

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:00.070
So, if you were to imagine

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:03.460
a prediction where
each data points

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:05.315
are follows its way down.

00:18:05.315 --> 00:18:06.730
In this case we're
going to look at

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:09.280
the path from the root to

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:10.760
the leaf corresponding to

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.445
the correct label for
that data points class.

00:18:14.445 --> 00:18:16.250
And so we're going
to look at all

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:17.510
the data points that reach

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:20.145
some corresponding
intermediate node

00:18:20.145 --> 00:18:23.225
along the path from the root
to its correct label.

00:18:23.225 --> 00:18:25.680
And that will give you
the distribution Q I.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.475
And obviously if
this distribution Q I

00:18:29.475 --> 00:18:33.340
is very peaked to one class
then you're basically there.

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:35.540
You basically know what
the right class is.

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:36.880
And if this isn't
really a uniform

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.745
distribution then
you're very uncertain.

00:18:38.745 --> 00:18:40.980
And your goal here of course,

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:42.410
just like with
learning any type of

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:45.480
decision tree is to make
this BSP desk possible.

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:46.600
Meaning you're making progress

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.555
towards figuring out
what the right class is.

00:18:48.555 --> 00:18:53.790
And so in this example
here we have four classes,

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.835
and this might be the root.

00:18:55.835 --> 00:18:59.740
And then we say
this Q just gives us

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:01.780
the distribution
of data points that

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:05.065
reached that corresponding
node with that class.

00:19:05.065 --> 00:19:07.290
Now, the next quantity
of relevance is

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:10.065
going to be measuring.

00:19:10.065 --> 00:19:12.700
Now that you know which nodes,

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:15.620
which examples which labels
reached this node,

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:17.630
where they sent to
next, all right?

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:20.020
Which child is or
is it going to?

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.050
And that's going
to be essential for

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:25.340
characterizing how good is
the splits at this node.

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:28.390
And for this we're
going to measure that by

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:32.420
this conditional
distribution P of J given I,

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:34.200
where the conditional we're

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.560
conditioning on examples
of each class I.

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:40.570
We're going to just look at
data points of some class,

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:42.850
and then ask what is
the distribution of

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:45.145
data points of that class
that go to each child.

00:19:45.145 --> 00:19:48.315
And intuitively again
we want this to be,

00:19:48.315 --> 00:19:50.330
we want each of these
conditional distributions

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:51.830
to be as peaked as possible

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:53.280
which means that
we're sort of routing

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.610
the data points
in an optimal way.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:58.110
And here where we're using

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.200
a softmax to make
those routing decisions,

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:04.055
this is just given to you by
the following expectation.

00:20:04.055 --> 00:20:09.195
And finally we can put
those two pieces together.

00:20:09.195 --> 00:20:13.685
P of J given I and Q of I to get

00:20:13.685 --> 00:20:18.025
P of J which is
simply the average,

00:20:18.025 --> 00:20:20.130
the fraction of
examples that go to

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.640
child I of this
corresponding node.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:26.490
Now, this is just a summary of

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:29.175
the three definitions
I gave you,

00:20:29.175 --> 00:20:30.900
and using those three
definitions now,

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:32.590
we have our overall objective

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.505
which is given on
the very bottom here.

00:20:34.505 --> 00:20:42.705
So, the objective for node n
is a sum over the Q I.

00:20:42.705 --> 00:20:46.105
Where again Q I is

00:20:46.105 --> 00:20:49.810
summing over all possible
classes or labels.

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:53.220
And then a sum over
the children of

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:58.810
that node of P J minus P
of J given I, all right?

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:02.400
And so, we want this to
be as high as possible

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:03.640
where as high as possible

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:06.645
means for this average quantity,

00:21:06.645 --> 00:21:10.490
we would like all of that mass

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:16.845
to be routed towards
a single child for each class.

00:21:16.845 --> 00:21:19.400
Now, this has a number
of properties.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.390
So, for example what
one can show is,

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:25.605
one can define this notion
of purity alpha n,

00:21:25.605 --> 00:21:28.380
and one can show that

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:33.765
this objective function
correlates with good purity.

00:21:33.765 --> 00:21:35.140
Namely, that if you were to

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:37.580
maximize this objective
as much as possible

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:42.445
it would lead toward
splits that are pure.

00:21:42.445 --> 00:21:44.870
Moreover, one could characterize

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:47.110
another notion of balance.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.200
How much are we balancing

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:55.785
across the classes
along the tree.

00:21:55.785 --> 00:21:58.490
And once again one can
show that you have to

00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:01.395
maximize this objective
as much as possible,

00:22:01.395 --> 00:22:05.020
it also leads to good balance.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:09.640
So, in our paper we have

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.290
some theoretical results which

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:15.840
motivate those two
properties I just gave.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:22.770
And also show that
there exist trees,

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:27.320
that are not too large,

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:30.342
that satisfy these properties.

00:22:30.342 --> 00:22:35.665
So, our overall learning
algorithm is then as follows.

00:22:35.665 --> 00:22:39.235
We're going to have-
this is going to be

00:22:39.235 --> 00:22:42.335
a batch SGD algorithm

00:22:42.335 --> 00:22:46.270
and for each batch size
of examples,

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:50.690
we're going to completely
redo the tree structure.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:53.255
And we're going to find- we have

00:22:53.255 --> 00:22:58.720
a greedy algorithm which
chooses the new tree structure,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.635
that is to say relabeling
each of the leaves with

00:23:01.635 --> 00:23:05.685
new classes that we show

00:23:05.685 --> 00:23:09.075
is greedily maximizing
the derivative

00:23:09.075 --> 00:23:11.275
of the sum of
these note objectives

00:23:11.275 --> 00:23:12.980
with respect to
our model parameters,

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:15.870
subject to well forming
these constraints.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:17.965
And so for each batch
we're going to

00:23:17.965 --> 00:23:20.465
first completely redo
the tree structure,

00:23:20.465 --> 00:23:23.035
then we're going to
take gradient steps with

00:23:23.035 --> 00:23:25.825
respect to the model parameters,

00:23:25.825 --> 00:23:27.775
so that recurrent network
the word embeddings

00:23:27.775 --> 00:23:30.790
and also the split parameters.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:33.635
Now, importantly each
one of these steps

00:23:33.635 --> 00:23:35.835
both choosing
the new tree structure and

00:23:35.835 --> 00:23:37.655
model optimizing
the parameters of

00:23:37.655 --> 00:23:39.095
your language model are all

00:23:39.095 --> 00:23:41.270
optimizing the same
objective function.

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:45.165
And so that really starts to
get at this goal of having

00:23:45.165 --> 00:23:48.465
a unified objective that
deals with both the fact that

00:23:48.465 --> 00:23:50.065
the representations
are changing and

00:23:50.065 --> 00:23:53.055
the fact that we want to
find really good trees.

00:23:53.055 --> 00:23:56.395
So, here's an example
of what it looks like.

00:23:56.395 --> 00:23:59.990
Suppose we see a new example
for this class was,

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:05.735
we look in the current tree
to see what leaf it is that.

00:24:05.735 --> 00:24:09.195
We compute the gradient of

00:24:09.195 --> 00:24:11.255
this objective for
every node along

00:24:11.255 --> 00:24:13.810
the path from the root
to its true label.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:16.720
We can update nodes statistics,

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:19.010
we take a gradient step

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:22.305
and then we update
the tree structure.

00:24:22.305 --> 00:24:26.125
And so here, you'll see as
global change like this,

00:24:26.125 --> 00:24:29.435
where pi moves from
the far right hand side.

00:24:29.435 --> 00:24:31.085
We do a new split,

00:24:31.085 --> 00:24:34.595
we get this new tree structure
and we iterate.

00:24:34.595 --> 00:24:39.770
So, in just the
remaining two minutes,

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:41.525
I'm going to talk a bit

00:24:41.525 --> 00:24:44.620
about how this algorithm
does in practice.

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:46.415
So, first we looked at language

00:24:46.415 --> 00:24:48.365
modelling problem where we took

00:24:48.365 --> 00:24:53.280
a large corpora from
the Gutenberg Project,

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:55.255
50 million word corpus.

00:24:55.255 --> 00:24:57.995
We use a vocabulary
of size 250,000,

00:24:57.995 --> 00:24:59.535
so there are 250,000 classes

00:24:59.535 --> 00:25:00.920
here we're trying to predict.

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:02.395
And for this work, we used

00:25:02.395 --> 00:25:03.945
a really simple log bi-linear

00:25:03.945 --> 00:25:05.735
n-gram neural language model.

00:25:05.735 --> 00:25:11.185
We learned m equals
65-ary tree of

00:25:11.185 --> 00:25:14.005
depth three and what

00:25:14.005 --> 00:25:16.745
I'm showing here is
a little bit hard to see,

00:25:16.745 --> 00:25:20.555
but what I'm showing is
the tree structure that's

00:25:20.555 --> 00:25:25.125
learned and visualized in
terms of what are the classes,

00:25:25.125 --> 00:25:26.795
the most frequent classes that

00:25:26.795 --> 00:25:28.355
are underneath each node of

00:25:28.355 --> 00:25:30.255
the tree to try to get

00:25:30.255 --> 00:25:32.575
a sense of whether
we're actually learning,

00:25:32.575 --> 00:25:35.775
whether there's any interesting
semantic grouping of

00:25:35.775 --> 00:25:36.995
the classes that's learned by

00:25:36.995 --> 00:25:39.155
this algorithm and
indeed we see that so.

00:25:39.155 --> 00:25:40.635
For example, on the far right we

00:25:40.635 --> 00:25:42.335
see that the class is this,

00:25:42.335 --> 00:25:45.230
these, those and so on
are grouped together.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:47.285
Over here we see
the classes put, set,

00:25:47.285 --> 00:25:50.175
lay, cut are grouped
together and so on.

00:25:50.175 --> 00:25:52.405
But, more importantly than

00:25:52.405 --> 00:25:54.420
the semantics is really
how it does in practice.

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:56.445
It's a real shame that
these plots aren't showing,

00:25:56.445 --> 00:25:59.490
but I'll walk you through
what they're saying.

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:02.015
This part on the left is

00:26:02.015 --> 00:26:05.185
the x-axis is epoch's
of training and

00:26:05.185 --> 00:26:08.035
the y-axis is perplexity
or how about like or

00:26:08.035 --> 00:26:09.595
a measure of how
about likelihood and

00:26:09.595 --> 00:26:12.090
which we want to be
as small as possible.

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:16.750
So, this red line that
you see up here is

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:23.220
the held-out perplexity
of using a random tree.

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:27.500
This blue line that you
can see pretty clearly

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:31.505
is the held-out perplexity
of using a flat softmax.

00:26:31.505 --> 00:26:33.965
So, it's going to be much
slower to train and use

00:26:33.965 --> 00:26:35.565
a test time because of

00:26:35.565 --> 00:26:37.385
the fact that it's not
hierarchically structured.

00:26:37.385 --> 00:26:40.280
And you see that there exists
a pretty big gap there.

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:42.745
Although, I'm not
showing you it here

00:26:42.745 --> 00:26:44.215
the clustering tree type of

00:26:44.215 --> 00:26:48.095
approach is actually worse
than all of these lines.

00:26:48.095 --> 00:26:51.790
The green line which is
almost impossible to see,

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:54.960
I'm going to show you it
by following my cursor.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:57.995
It initially begins
by somewhat being

00:26:57.995 --> 00:27:01.400
similar to the random hierarchy,

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:04.235
until roughly the second epoch
of training where it

00:27:04.235 --> 00:27:07.670
settles on a good
tree structure.

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:09.755
And from there on out,

00:27:09.755 --> 00:27:11.825
it's learning curve is identical

00:27:11.825 --> 00:27:13.740
to that of the flat softmax.

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:15.825
And so what we see here is

00:27:15.825 --> 00:27:17.895
that by learning
the tree structure,

00:27:17.895 --> 00:27:20.290
in fact we're able
to fully recover

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:22.665
the quality of model
that we're able to

00:27:22.665 --> 00:27:26.105
get using just a flat softmax.

00:27:26.105 --> 00:27:28.895
Next, we looked at
a text classification

00:27:28.895 --> 00:27:31.935
problem where we actually
modeled it almost identically

00:27:31.935 --> 00:27:35.475
to the density estimation
setting previously considered,

00:27:35.475 --> 00:27:38.800
with only difference being
that here to do prediction,

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:40.615
we don't want to sample we

00:27:40.615 --> 00:27:42.440
actually want to
do map inference.

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.025
So, for prediction
here, we're going to be

00:27:44.025 --> 00:27:50.160
doing a map inference for

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:53.755
the class which we do by

00:27:53.755 --> 00:27:55.615
simple branch and bound with

00:27:55.615 --> 00:27:58.775
a depth-first search through
this hierarchical tree,

00:27:58.775 --> 00:28:00.835
in order to predict
for each new example.

00:28:00.835 --> 00:28:04.415
We evaluated on this flicker
type classification setting,

00:28:04.415 --> 00:28:06.195
where the input is an image like

00:28:06.195 --> 00:28:07.880
this and your goal
is to figure out

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:11.695
what are the tags that
this input corresponds to.

00:28:11.695 --> 00:28:13.885
Now most people use this dataset

00:28:13.885 --> 00:28:15.885
to try to predict
the tag directly

00:28:15.885 --> 00:28:17.575
from the image or to
do something about

00:28:17.575 --> 00:28:19.890
relating damage to the caption.

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:22.935
I want to first
out that we could

00:28:22.935 --> 00:28:26.145
have used this approach from
the image itself. All right.

00:28:26.145 --> 00:28:28.205
So, one could imagine using

00:28:28.205 --> 00:28:30.285
our algorithm to
simultaneously learn

00:28:30.285 --> 00:28:33.105
the parameters of
a convolutional neural network

00:28:33.105 --> 00:28:36.440
and a hierarchical softmax
to predict the class.

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:39.090
That would be a good use case.

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:40.745
We wanted to stick with

00:28:40.745 --> 00:28:42.445
language because there's
something that my student is

00:28:42.445 --> 00:28:44.095
more interested in
and so we didn't

00:28:44.095 --> 00:28:45.965
actually use the image
itself rather we use

00:28:45.965 --> 00:28:47.725
the text of
the caption and ask we

00:28:47.725 --> 00:28:51.325
predict the tags from
the texts of the caption,

00:28:51.325 --> 00:28:53.960
using a simple bag-of-words

00:28:53.960 --> 00:28:55.505
embedding where
we're simultaneously

00:28:55.505 --> 00:28:57.830
learning the word embeddings
at the same time.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:00.530
And indeed the same story
applies here,

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:03.560
where the learned tree
that I'm showing you here,

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:11.070
here for the areas 5 and 20
obtains precision at one,

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:12.375
which is substantially better

00:29:12.375 --> 00:29:13.255
than you could have gotten with

00:29:13.255 --> 00:29:15.205
a Huffman tree or a couple of

00:29:15.205 --> 00:29:18.930
other baseline algorithms
that we compare to.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:22.805
What's particularly
interesting however is that,

00:29:22.805 --> 00:29:24.365
as the dimensionality
of your word

00:29:24.365 --> 00:29:27.010
embeddings increases
that gap seems to shrink.

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:29.075
So, I think that's

00:29:29.075 --> 00:29:30.385
something that'll be interesting

00:29:30.385 --> 00:29:32.100
for us to think
about in the future.

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.530
In particular what
this motivates is that,

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:38.540
maybe this approach of
learning hierarchical softmax,

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:41.495
we're learning the structure
maybe that's most

00:29:41.495 --> 00:29:42.775
important in settings where

00:29:42.775 --> 00:29:44.860
you're more resource
constrained.

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:47.085
Where you can't afford to have

00:29:47.085 --> 00:29:49.375
very large vocabularies and

00:29:49.375 --> 00:29:51.625
so the decisions
that are- sorry,

00:29:51.625 --> 00:29:53.460
you can't afford to have
very large dimensionalities

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:55.185
and so the decisions
that are made at

00:29:55.185 --> 00:29:59.540
each step of the tree are
hard and because they're hard,

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:02.705
learning the tree structure
is more important.

00:30:02.705 --> 00:30:05.685
So, I'll just conclude by
saying this is one of the

00:30:05.685 --> 00:30:07.925
first works that show how to

00:30:07.925 --> 00:30:09.875
learn the tree structure of

00:30:09.875 --> 00:30:11.275
hierarchical softmax together

00:30:11.275 --> 00:30:13.215
with the underlying
representations.

00:30:13.215 --> 00:30:14.855
There are ton of open questions

00:30:14.855 --> 00:30:16.855
particularly on
the theoretical side and

00:30:16.855 --> 00:30:19.155
there are also some
interesting empirical work

00:30:19.155 --> 00:30:20.765
to be done and
some new directions there.

00:30:20.765 --> 00:30:23.250
So, just to spear some interest,

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:25.025
there's really
no reason why we had

00:30:25.025 --> 00:30:26.245
to use a tree-structured model.

00:30:26.245 --> 00:30:28.110
One could imagine
using a forest,

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:31.825
one could imagine allowing

00:30:31.825 --> 00:30:35.755
each class to appear
a couple of times

00:30:35.755 --> 00:30:37.915
rather than just
once in the tree

00:30:37.915 --> 00:30:39.745
and one could even
imagine using the sort of

00:30:39.745 --> 00:30:41.425
same structure
learning algorithm for

00:30:41.425 --> 00:30:46.305
other deep learning
applications. So, thank you.

00:30:49.385 --> 00:30:53.085
&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you. So, we have

00:30:53.085 --> 00:30:54.535
maybe time only for

00:30:54.535 --> 00:30:58.375
one or two quick questions
if there are any.

00:30:58.445 --> 00:31:04.955
Yeah maybe this
one and then yeah.

00:31:08.235 --> 00:31:11.845
&gt;&gt; So the question
is about applying

00:31:11.845 --> 00:31:16.245
hierarchical softmax for
multi-label classification.

00:31:21.565 --> 00:31:25.915
&gt;&gt; Naively as could
be used there as well.

00:31:25.915 --> 00:31:29.755
I don't know of
the best way to do it.

00:31:29.755 --> 00:31:30.935
I had to think about it.

00:31:30.935 --> 00:31:32.165
That's a really
interesting question.

00:31:32.165 --> 00:31:36.475
David's asking whether
the structure could be reused

00:31:36.475 --> 00:31:38.675
or whatever original thoughts
is that the structure

00:31:38.675 --> 00:31:39.845
itself might be interesting

00:31:39.845 --> 00:31:41.215
from a linguistic point of view.

00:31:41.215 --> 00:31:43.340
Particularly on
language modeling setting.

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:45.225
I don't think
there's evidence from

00:31:45.225 --> 00:31:48.105
extra results to
justify that statement.

00:31:48.105 --> 00:31:49.665
A second question is

00:31:49.665 --> 00:31:51.045
whether the structure
could be reused for

00:31:51.045 --> 00:31:55.955
other predictive problems and I

00:31:55.955 --> 00:31:59.955
think the short answer
is yes if the way that

00:31:59.955 --> 00:32:03.735
the representations relate to

00:32:03.735 --> 00:32:06.865
the labels that you're
predicting is generalizable.

00:32:06.865 --> 00:32:13.305
&gt;&gt; Okay. Let's
thank our speaker.

